# src/vendor/phpmailer/.htaccess — DENY EVERYTHING IN THIS DIRECTORY.
#
# The three files beside this one are third-party library code. They are
# `require`d by ../../contact-send.php and are never a URL anybody should be
# able to fetch. Requesting PHPMailer.php directly today produces an empty
# 200 — the file only declares a namespace and a class, so nothing runs and
# nothing prints — but an empty 200 still confirms to a scanner that this
# library is present at this path, and the version constant is two lines into
# a file that a future misconfiguration (a lost handler mapping, a rolled-back
# .htaccess) would serve as plain text. Denying the directory outright costs
# nothing and closes both, today and after any such rollback.
#
# THIS FILE IS PER-DIRECTORY ON PURPOSE rather than a fifth name added to the
# FilesMatch block in ../../.htaccess. That block guards files whose names are
# fixed by the control panel; this one guards a whole directory whose contents
# change whenever the library is updated, and keeping the rule inside the
# directory means a future file added here is denied the moment it lands
# instead of the moment somebody remembers to extend a regular expression two
# levels up.
#
# BOTH AUTHORISATION SYNTAXES, EACH MODULE-GUARDED, exactly as ../../.htaccess
# argues at its own deny block: an unguarded directive for an absent module
# returns 500 for the whole subtree (T-02-02). The modern syntax is tried
# first; the legacy one fires only where mod_authz_core is genuinely absent,
# so the two can never both apply.
#
# T-04-24 (the vendored library silently swapped) is mitigated by the SHA256
# digests recorded in 04-05-SUMMARY.md, not by this file. This file only stops
# the copy being READ over HTTP.
<IfModule mod_authz_core.c>
	Require all denied
</IfModule>
<IfModule !mod_authz_core.c>
	<IfModule mod_access_compat.c>
		Order allow,deny
		Deny from all
	</IfModule>
</IfModule>
